<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Italian Eritrea</h1><p class="st-meta">1890 CE – 1941 CE · capital Asmara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Italy's first colony, carved from the Red Sea coast and highlands, which gave Eritrea its borders, its capital Asmara and a distinct identity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>An Italian shipping company bought the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assab-bay">Assab</a> in 1869, and the Italian state took it over in 1882. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> in 1885 with British approval and pushed inland, despite defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dogali">Dogali</a> in 1887. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> in 1889 recognised its gains, and on 1 January 1890 the colony of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-eritrea">Eritrea</a> was proclaimed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy's</a> defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896 fixed the border with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Under governors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-martini">Ferdinando Martini</a>, the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a>, which was rebuilt with Italian modernist architecture. A railway linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> to Asmara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agordat">Agordat</a>, and tens of thousands of Eritrean askari served in Italy's colonial wars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">Racial segregation</a> was imposed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1936 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-eritrea">Eritrea</a> became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-east-africa">Italian East Africa</a>. British forces took it in 1941 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-keren">Battle of Keren</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-administration-of-eritrea">British Administration of Eritrea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara's</a> Italian-era centre is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/italian-eritrea.jpg" alt="Italian Eritrea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy_(1861-1946)_crowned.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1890 CE – 1941 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Asmara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ferdinando Martini</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tigrinya, Tigre, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Eritrea</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colonia Eritrea, Eritrea Colony</dd></dl></aside></div>
